<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herrnhut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1722">1722</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herrnhut is a small town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, founded in 1722 by Protestant refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> on the estate of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-ludwig-von-zinzendorf">Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf</a>. It became the centre of the renewed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Church</a>, whose missionaries spread worldwide. The Moravian 'Daily Watchwords' have been published there since 1731, and the town is known for its star-shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advent">Advent</a> lanterns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herrnhut.jpg" alt="Herrnhut" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: heyeje · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siedlung_Herrnhut_mit_Gottesacker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Herrnhut lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, in the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, in the hilly foreland of the Zittau Mountains on the Petersbach, the longest headstream of the Pließnitz, a tributary of the Lusatian Neisse. It is about 10 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a>, 15 km north-west of Zittau and 25 km south-west of Görlitz. The name Herrn Hut means the Lord's watchful care. The hill above the town, the Hutberg or Hill of Watching, suggested it, and the town's coat of arms shows the tower of the lookout on the Hutberg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Unity of the Brethren arose after the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a> was burned at the stake at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> in 1415. In the 18th century its members in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Lands of the Bohemian Crown</a> faced the harsh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> measures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rulers. From 1722 refugees came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> had held since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> of 1635. After meeting their leader Christian David, a Moravian missionary, Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-ludwig-von-zinzendorf">Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf</a> (1700–1760) invited them to settle on his Berthelsdorf estates. David built the first house in what became Herrnhut, and Zinzendorf had a new residence built there in 1725–1727.</p><p>The community spread from Herrnhut to Rixdorf near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, to Marienborn near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budingen">Büdingen</a>, to Herrnhaag and Norden. The first organised Protestant missionary movement began from Herrnhut in 1732, when two Bohemian Brethren went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a>; others went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>. The first North American mission began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, in 1735, and the mission station of Genadendal was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1738. Zinzendorf's community influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wesley">John Wesley</a> in creating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodism</a> and contributed to the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evangelicalism">Evangelicalism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Herrnhut is a town of around 6,000 inhabitants and the centre of the worldwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Church</a>, the Unitas Fratrum. It makes the Herrnhuter Stern, a 25-pointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advent">Advent</a> star that has been produced for over 150 years and is hung in windows and on porches at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. In 2016 the town received the title European City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and in 2024 it was inscribed on the World Heritage List with Christiansfeld in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> in the US and Gracehill in Northern Ireland as the serial site Moravian Church Settlements.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herrnhut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The worldwide Moravian Church takes its name from Brethren refugees who left Moravia for <b>Herrnhut</b> in Saxony in 1722; its missionaries reached the Caribbean by 1732.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Georg Schmidt, a Moravian Brother from <b>Herrnhut</b> in Saxony, founded Genadendal in 1738 among the Khoe-speaking peoples at Baviaanskloof in the Riviersonderend Mountains, the first mission station in southern Africa, and the Griqua states founded by the Kok dynasty arguably answered the influence of missionaries.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>…a Saxon noble who had studied under the Pietist leader August Hermann Francke, invited members of the Moravian Church to his estates in 1722, where they founded a community at <b>Herrnhut</b>; it became a refuge for other Protestants as well, and Moravian societies were later established in England.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>A settlement of Hussites at <b>Herrnhut</b> in Saxony in 1722 caused the emergence of the Moravian Church.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hussites">Hussites · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The mystic Jakob Böhme lived in Görlitz, and in 1722 Moravian refugees founded <b>Herrnhut</b> under Count Zinzendorf.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-upper-lusatia">Margraviate of Upper Lusatia</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf, founder of the renewed Moravian Church at <b>Herrnhut</b>, married Erdmuthe Dorothea of Reuss-Ebersdorf in 1722, and a Moravian congregation settled in Ebersdorf.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-ebersdorf">Principality of Reuss-Ebersdorf</a></li></ul></section>
